 practice run in rugged mountains in Albania.\nThe chopper may have run into a power line, CNN reported. It was the second Apache crash in nine days, even though none of the $14.5 million state-of-the-art choppers has flown any combat missions.\nThe Pentagon insisted “this doesn’t alter any plans” to use the remaining 22 helicopters in Kosovo, although no combat order has been triggered.\nAt Spangdahlem, Clinton, dressed in a brown leather bomber jacket, posed with the beaming POWs.\n“I’m proud of them,” he said. He also hinted he’d soon OK the release of the two Serbian soldiers captured by the rebel Kosovo Liberation Army and handed over to the allied forces.\nPentagon boss William Cohen denied the move was a payback to Yugoslavia’s President Slobodan Milosevic for freeing the GIs.\n“This is not a goodwill overture to Mr. Milosevic,” he said sharply.\nEarlier, Clinton huddled with NATO honchos in Brussels on possible ground-troop scenarios. The Wall Street Journal reported that top NATO military strategists have come up with one plan that, if implemented, would send 60,000 troops into a “semi-permissive” Kosovo in late July.\n\n# PREZ PRAISES ‘BRAVE AMERICANS’ KILLED IN CRASH\nA Clinton national security spokesman disputed the newspaper report and stressed: “We do not support a NATO ground force in a hypothetical semi-permissive environment.”\nWhite House officials acknowledge it will take more than the 28,000 peacekeeping troops originally envisioned to stabilize post-war Kosovo. They insisted no new figure has been finalized.\nAs for the fatal chopper crash, military officials said the two crew members – described as well-trained pilots, although only one was actually flying the chopper – were dead when a rescue team arrived at the scene 15 minutes after the explosion.\nLast week, two other Apache crewmen escaped with cuts and bruises after they crashed, reportedly after hitting a tree, during a similar nighttime training mission near the Kosovo border.\nSince 1985, there have been 14 fatalities involving Apaches.\nAt the Pentagon, Maj. Gen. Charles Wald said the two crashes were unrelated.\n“There is no specific problem … They’re training hard in terrain that they’re not used to flying in … under tough conditions … Whether it’s in training or operations, this is dangerous business,” he said.\nIn Germany, Clinton told the U.S. airmen, some of whom were involved in the Bosnia conflict: “I know this is hard. I know too many of these pilots are flying long hours with too little rest. I know the stress and anxiety must be unbearable.\n“I’m sorry you have to do it all over again,” Clinton said as he reminded them that they were fighting for “the common humanity of every breathing, living person in this continent.”\nIn other developments yesterday, a Greek relief convoy carrying medicine from Macedonia to a hospital in Pristina, the capital of Kosovo, was hit by a bomb. No one was injured. The Pentagon denied allied warplanes were anywhere near the convoy.\nAs NATO kept pounding Yugoslavia, Kosovo Albanian leader Ibrahim Rugova, thought to be under house arrest, flew to Rome to promote a diplomatic end to the crisis.\nRead NextDIAL P FOR PAULA – TELEPHONE PSYCHIC\nSPONSORED STORIES\n\n# PREZ PRAISES ‘BRAVE AMERICANS’ KILLED IN CRASH\n#### Trending Now  in US News\n*   This story has been shared 43,260 times.43,260   ### ‘Finance bros’ torturing NYC neighborhood with wild rooftop parties featuring dozens of revelers   \n*   This story has been shared 35,450 times.35,450   ### Mamdani disbands NYPD’s controversial unit, announces new ‘civilian-led approach’ to protests   \n*   This story has been shared 30,407 times.30,407   ### Horrifying torture details released about legendary California surfer’s killing after girlfriend’s arrest   \n*   This story has been shared 24,191 times.24,191   ### Small-town teacher breaks down as she admits sex with student, 16, she shared nudes with\n\n# PREZ PRAISES ‘BRAVE AMERICANS’ KILLED IN CRASH